<p><strong>Discounts leak when anyone can give any amount and nobody sees it until the month ends. The fix is a limit staff cannot pass, an approval for anything above it, and a report the owner can read.</strong></p>
<p><em>The scene below is a typical one, not a single named customer.</em></p>
<h2>The total is lower and nobody knows why</h2>
<p>Sales look fine, but the cash at the end of the month is lower than it should be. A receptionist gave a friend 30% off. A therapist rounded a bill down for a regular. Each one is small. Together they are a salary.</p>
<h2>Why this happens</h2>
<ul><li>Anyone can type any discount.</li><li>Nothing is recorded as a discount, only a lower price.</li><li>The owner is not there to say no.</li></ul>
<h2>How to control discounts</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Set a limit by role.</strong> A receptionist can give 5%, a manager 15%, and the owner anything.</li><li><strong>Require approval above the limit.</strong></li><li><strong>Record every discount</strong> with who gave it and why.</li><li><strong>Read the discounts by staff member</strong> once a week.</li></ol>
<h2>How Zennly helps</h2>
<p>Zennly has <a href="/features/discount-management/">discount control</a> with approvals, tied to <a href="/features/role-based-access/">role-based access</a> so each person sees and does only what their role allows. Discounts are recorded, and <a href="/features/reports/">reports</a> show who gave them, so an owner away from the branch can still see where money went.</p>
<h2>A quick fix you can start today</h2>
<ol><li>Decide the most each role may give.</li><li>Tell the team that anything above needs your yes.</li><li>Write every discount down with a reason, even on paper.</li></ol>
